"Accelerating scientific discovery with AI-powered Empirical Research Assistance (https://research.google/blog/accelerating-scientific-discovery-with-ai-powered-empirical-software/)\nciteturn29808search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: today; September 9, 2025Lizzie Dorfman, Product Manager, and Michael Brenner, Research Scientist, Google Research\n\nImage: AI-powered-empirical software-overview\n\n# Accelerating scientific discovery with AI-powered Empirical Research Assistance\n\nSeptember 9, 2025\n\nLizzie Dorfman, Product Manager, and Michael Brenner, Research Scientist, Google Research\n\nUpdate • apr 29, 2026\n\nThis post has been updated to include the newly released system name, Empirical Research Assistance.\n\nOur new AI system, Empirical Research Assistance, helps scientists write empirical software, achieving expert-level results on six diverse, challenging problems.\n\nIn scientific research, thoroughly evaluating hypotheses is essential to developing more robust and comprehensive answers, but the required work forms a bottleneck, hindering the pace of discovery. In particular, much of modern scientific research depends on computational experiments to model, simulate, and analyze complex phenomena. Here, hypothesis evaluation often requires creating custom software, a slow and challenging task. Given the increasing capability of large language models (LLMs) to perform traditional coding tasks, we wondered if they could similarly generate high-quality custom software for evaluating and iteratively improving scientific hypotheses.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVijaye Raji to become CTO of Applications with acquisition of Statsig | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/vijaye-raji-to-become-cto-of-applications-with-acquisition-of-statsig/)\nciteturn29808search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; September 2, 2025 ... We’re expanding our Applications leadership, the org responsible for how our research reaches and benefits the world.\n\nSeptember 2, 2025\n\nCompany\n\n# Vijaye Raji to become CTO of Applications with acquisition of Statsig\n\nWe’re expanding our Applications leadership, the org responsible for how our research reaches and benefits the world.\n\nAs we scale ChatGPT and build new applications to serve hundreds of millions of people and businesses around the world, our ambition is to push the frontier of AI research and turn it into intuitive, safe, and useful tools that people love. That takes strong engineering systems, fast iteration, and a long-term focus on quality and reliability.\n\nVijaye Raji will step into a new role as CTO of Applications⁠, reporting to Fidji Simo, following the acquisition of Statsig. As a hands-on builder and trusted leader, Vijaye will head product engineering for ChatGPT and Codex, with responsibilities that span core systems and product lines including infrastructure and Integrity. His experience as founder and CEO of Statsig, and a decade leading large-scale consumer engineering at Meta, brings both entrepreneurial vision and operating expertise to scale our next generation of products.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBuilding more helpful ChatGPT experiences for everyone | OpenAI (https://openai.com/index/building-more-helpful-chatgpt-experiences-for-everyone/)\nciteturn29808search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: today; September 2, 2025 ... Their input will help us define and measure well-being, set priorities, and design future safeguards—such as future iterations of parental controls—with the latest research in mind.\n\nOpenAI\n\nSeptember 2, 2025\n\nSafetyProduct\n\n# Building more helpful ChatGPT experiences for everyone\n\nRouting sensitive conversations to reasoning models and rolling out Parental Controls within the next month.\n\nOur work to make ChatGPT as helpful as possible is constant and ongoing. We’ve seen people turn to it in the most difficult of moments. That’s why we continue to improve how our models recognize and respond to signs of mental and emotional distress, guided by expert input. \n\nThis work has already been underway, but we want to proactively preview our plans for the next 120 days, so you won’t need to wait for launches to see where we’re headed. The work will continue well beyond this period of time, but we’re making a focused effort to launch as many of these improvements as possible this year. \n\nLast week, we shared four focus areas when it comes to helping people when they need it most:\n\n  1. Expanding interventions to more people in crisis\n\n  2. Making it even easier to reach emergency services and get help from experts\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKruel.ai KV2.0 - KX (experimental research) to current 8.2- Api companion co-pilot system with full modality , understanding with persistent memory - #419 by darcschnider - Community - OpenAI Developer Community (https://community.openai.com/t/kruel-ai-kv2-0-kx-experimental-research-to-current-8-2-api-companion-co-pilot-system-with-full-modality-understanding-with-persistent-memory/674592/419)\nciteturn29808search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.1 years ago; Crawled: last year; # Kruel.ai KV2.0 - KX (experimental research) to current 8.2- Api companion co-pilot system with full modality , understanding with persistent memory ... darcschnider September 2, 2025, 11:06pm 419\n\n# Kruel.ai KV2.0 - KX (experimental research) to current 8.2- Api companion co-pilot system with full modality , understanding with persistent memory\n\nCommunity\n\nproject\n\ndarcschnider September 2, 2025, 11:06pm 419\n\nImage Medium – 2 Sep 25 Image\n\n### Envisioning a Multimodal AI World\n\nArtificial intelligence is evolving AGI is getting closer with this next step, along with Memory (persist with current information)\n\nReading time: 10 min re